<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugou Bridge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1189">1189</a>–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Marco Polo Bridge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lugou Bridge, known in the West as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> Bridge, is a granite bridge of eleven arches over the Yongding River south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, built under the Jin dynasty between 1189 and 1192. Its balustrades carry hundreds of carved stone lions, and Marco Polo described it in his travels. A clash between Chinese and Japanese troops there on 7 July 1937 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge.jpg" alt="Lugou Bridge" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alanba42 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lugouqiao-2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its move of the capital to Zhongdu began Beijing's history as a main imperial capital, a role continued by the Mongol Yuan, the Ming and the Qing; the <b>Lugou Bridge</b>, known in the West as the Marco Polo Bridge, was built near Zhongdu between 1189 and 1192.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From it Japan launched the invasion of the rest of China after the <b>Marco Polo Bridge</b> incident of 1937, and in May 1939 a clash between Manchukuoan and Mongolian cavalry grew into the Battles of Khalkhin Gol, in which a combined Soviet–Mongolian force defeated the Kwantung Army with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A night-time clash between Japanese and Chinese troops at the <b>Marco Polo Bridge</b> near Beijing escalated into full-scale war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1937, soon after the <b>Marco Polo Bridge</b> incident, its own Chinese security troops mutinied at Tongzhou and killed many Japanese and Korean residents.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-hebei-autonomous-government">East Hebei Autonomous Government</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Marco Polo Bridge</b> incident of July 1937, the Japanese North China Area Army occupied Beijing and Tianjin and pushed south.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Provisional Government of the Republic of China</a></li></ul></section>
